      "- **Day 1:** if they haven't booked, a friendly text: 'Hi Sarah, it's Mike from Northline Roofing. Still happy to come by for a look at your roof. Would Thursday or Friday afternoon work?'",
      "- **Day 3:** a short email with something useful, like what to expect during an estimate, plus a link to book.",
      "- **Day 7:** a final, low-pressure text: 'Just checking in. If now isn't the right time, no problem. Reply anytime and we'll set something up.'",
      "The moment they reply or book, the sequence stops automatically and a person takes over.",
      "## Follow-up after estimates",
      "Another common gap is after the estimate. You've visited, quoted, and then heard nothing. A post-estimate sequence might look like this:",
      "- **Day 2:** 'Thanks for having us out. Any questions about the quote? Happy to walk through it.'",
      "- **Day 7:** a short note addressing a common concern, like financing options or scheduling availability.",
      "- **Day 14:** a final check-in before the quote's expiry date.",
      "Homeowners comparing three quotes often go with the contractor who stayed in touch and answered questions, not necessarily the cheapest.",
      "## Rules for follow-up that works",
      "### Sound like a person",
      "Write in your own voice, use their name, and refer to the actual job. Generic marketing messages get ignored.",
      "### Mix text and email",
      "Text gets seen quickly and suits short check-ins. Email suits longer information. Most good sequences use both.",
      "### Be helpful, not pushy",
