      "### Know when to stop",
      "Three to five messages over a week or two is plenty for most leads. After that, move them to an occasional, helpful newsletter instead. See [are email newsletters worth it](/blog/are-email-newsletters-worth-it).",
      "### Stop when they respond",
      "Nothing is worse than an automated 'just checking in' text arriving after someone has already booked. Make sure replies and bookings stop the sequence immediately.",
      "### Follow the rules",
      "Following up with someone who contacted you about a job is generally covered by implied consent under Canada's Anti-Spam Legislation, which lasts six months after an inquiry. Identify your business and offer an easy way to opt out, and keep marketing messages for people who've agreed to receive them.",
      "![A contractor reading a homeowner's reply to a follow-up text while standing beside his truck](/blog/img/automated-follow-up-2.webp \"A short, friendly check-in is often all it takes to bring a quiet lead back.\")",
      "## What it looks like in your day",
